<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">15 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 15 May, from 392 to 1957.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">392 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,634 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Valentinian II is found hanged at Vienne</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The young western emperor was found dead in his residence in Gaul. Whether he was murdered by his general Arbogast or took his own life is still debated; civil war followed.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1252 CE <span class="tih-ago">774 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Innocent IV authorises torture by inquisitors</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The bull Ad extirpanda permitted secular authorities to use torture to extract confessions from suspected heretics. It set a precedent for inquisitorial practice for centuries.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/england" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/england.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1602 CE <span class="tih-ago">424 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bartholomew Gosnold names Cape Cod</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">English navigator Bartholomew Gosnold reached the Massachusetts peninsula and named it for the abundant cod his crew caught there. His voyage was an early English exploration of New England.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1618 CE <span class="tih-ago">408 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Johannes Kepler confirms his third law of planetary motion</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Kepler found that the square of a planet's orbital period is proportional to the cube of its distance from the Sun. Published in Harmonices Mundi, the law later underpinned Newton's theory of gravitation.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1940 CE <span class="tih-ago">86 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nylon stockings go on sale across the United States</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Shoppers bought some four million pairs of DuPont nylon stockings on the first day of nationwide sales. The synthetic fibre transformed fashion and was soon diverted to wartime parachutes.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1957 CE <span class="tih-ago">69 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Britain tests its first thermonuclear weapon design</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Operation Grapple saw a British bomb dropped near Malden Island in the Pacific. Its yield fell short of a true hydrogen bomb, but later tests that year succeeded and secured US nuclear cooperation.</p></div></li></ol>
